{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'served ledger|served store|items-comparison.sqlite' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers -g 'codex-r72*.md' | head -n 13; ls -l poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/views.sqlite; nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '104,176p'\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r72c.md:3:2. **§3 classified the stored questions correctly.** I independently found 19, 17 and 18 number questions in the three comparison stores. In the sealed ledger, withholding covered 43 money questions and 21 other numeric questions. All 30 Chinese Yes/No pairs were recognized. The Xbox question correctly kept its Yes/No answer despite having bounds around zero. One distinction matters for release prose. **All 85 comparison-store events are also Yes/No questions**, including both known leaks. The §4 prose rule must apply to them even when the event check matches first. The served ledger also has a binary event labeled “Restriction adopted” and “No restriction adopted.” Its existing answer label should remain usable under the same prose rule. [Design §3:65](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:65) · [vocabulary.py:24](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/vocabulary.py:24) · [test_service.py:90](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_service.py:90) · [named event decision:3510](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3510)\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  19767296 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/views.sqlite\n   104\t3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path passes, and whether\n   105\t   the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.\n   106\t4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is quoted in the\n   107\t   design and in the question to Alex.\n   108\t\n   109\t- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not judged. Enumerate\n   110\t  the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.\n   111\t- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\n   112\t  and whether that decision is identity (\"are these the same thing?\") or judgement (\"does this matter?\"); build only the identity part.\n   113\t  A list of what matters is at most a hint shown to the model (Alex, 2026-10-25: \"你这个做法难道不是非常的限死吗？永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\").\n   114\t- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n   115\t  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the\n   116\t  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: \"我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix\").\n   117\t- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,\n   118\t  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a\n   119\t  question, not something to build.\n   120\t- A foundation's acceptance carries a size and speed budget from its first control, not only correctness.\n   121\t\n   122\t## 3. Writing code\n   123\t\n   124\t- **One owner.** A rule lives where the data is written, and refuses: a warning that lets the run continue is a silent failure. Fix the\n   125\t  writer and delete its duplicate implementations in the same change; when a fix lands in one consumer, search every consumer of the\n   126\t  same key or rule.\n   127\t- **Code over prose.** A contract that code or a schema can enforce (an enum over allowed keys, a shape) is enforced there, and stays out\n   128\t  of prompts and documents.\n   129\t- **One word per concept.** The glossary in `docs/prd/00-system.md` (section Glossary; D36, D163) names each concept once, and\n   130\t  `docs/check.py` fails on a word it rules out. A new concept gets its row and its ruled-out words in the same change; a new ruled-out\n   131\t  word must catch the sentence it was written against and pass the corrected one.\n   132\t- **Identity.** An identifier names one thing for the life of its scope; a changed thing gets a new id. Equal values alone never\n   133\t  establish identity (D248 boundary 7, the same statement carried by several outlets, is a chosen exception, switched on only after its\n   134\t  merge risk on the gold and the accuracy of the original speaker and day fields are measured).\n   135\t- **Explicit inputs.** Library code takes runtime paths and configuration as arguments. A branch a run takes comes from its\n   136\t  configuration, never from whether an environment variable happens to be set; a configured step whose credential is missing refuses\n   137\t  the run. A component that sends a model call uses the transport its caller passes.\n   138\t- **Never order by the wall clock.** It steps both ways; order comes from a sequence the program owns: dispatch's sequence number. The\n   139\t  stream never reads the calendar; only pull mode acts on time, with its \"now\" passed in (D12, D13, D31).\n   140\t- **Strictness is code too.** A check added to prevent defects can itself fail; accept it on real data, not samples.\n   141\t- **Style.** Descriptive names. Docstrings and comments state behaviour, not history. The standard library unless a measured need\n   142\t  justifies a dependency.\n   143\t- **Frontend presentation.** Follow the sentence-case and metadata-separator rules in `docs/poc/thesis-ui.md#presentation-rules`.\n   144\t\n   145\t## 4. Tests\n   146\t\n   147\t- Every test names the requirement or table row it proves; a test that traces to none is deleted, and a retired behaviour's tests leave\n   148\t  with it. The suite stays lean.\n   149\t- Before writing a test, read every function its fixture drives: return shapes, failure classification, the data and attributes it\n   150\t  reads. Check that a name exists before calling it.\n   151\t- Real objects first (real storage on a temporary path, real records). Fake only external services (a model client, a data source, an\n   152\t  HTTP server). Fixture values are realistic (real ISO timestamps, not placeholders).\n   153\t- Tests never reach a paid service or load a credential: `tests/conftest.py` replaces the model and Jev key readers with a refusal, so a\n   154\t  call that escapes its fake transport fails the test.\n   155\t- Write the one decisive assertion that fails without the code under test, and predict the run before running it. A first failure for\n   156\t  any other reason means the test was written blind: stop and read again. A test is never mended to fit the code; it changes only when\n   157\t  the spec changes, and the spec change is recorded first.\n   158\t- Mutate on a scratch copy: the test must fail on the mutant while the unmutated control passes. An error is not a catch until its\n   159\t  traceback is read. A negative assertion (\"never called\") needs a mutant that makes the call on the tested path.\n   160\t- A check must judge something: it reports how many cases it judged, carries a positive control that fails for the breach it holds, and\n   161\t  fails when its own preconditions fail (exit status, a missing or unidentifiable input). A check over an empty population is\n   162\t  unexercised, not passed.\n   163\t- Tests never depend on the real date or the working directory: pin every date written and read, build paths from the test file's\n   164\t  location, write only inside temporary directories.\n   165\t- Test each entry point as its user calls it (with the defaults the user leaves unset, under the configuration the user runs), not only\n   166\t  the inner function with every argument set.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 5. Verification\n   169\t\n   170\tVerify at the point the change touches, at the sharpest level that shows it works or fails, and stop there:\n   171\t1. Stored data: reproduce the failing case, show the fix flips it, show a mutant of the fix fails the check ($0).\n   172\t2. Only when what the model is asked changed: re-send just the affected calls.\n   173\t3. The touched suites, plus any standing structural checks the change can affect; the full suite once.\n   174\t4. The gate once, at merge (README, \"Build and check\"). The acceptance once, on the final configuration, scored on the frozen ruler\n   175\t   (§7.1). Never a full rerun per fix.\n   176\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
